# Does reopening OBS's audio source reset OBS's own A/V offset? (host, 2026-11-26)
# Run: tests/logs/20261126-040051_6.8.0-151-generic_live.log (live.sh obssoak in local mode,
# 600 s, one test-source session, a change every 60 s: idle, reopen-video, idle, reopen-audio,
# idle, reopen-video, reopen-audio, idle, idle). reopen-audio: custom_pcm toggled between
# hw:CARD=/dev/snd/by-path/pci-0000:07:00.0,DEV=0 and the same without ,DEV=0 (OBS closes
# and opens the PCM). The recording was copied before live.sh deleted it, analysed with
# tests/obs-av.py's video() and audio() (flash box 0.70,0.88,0.92,0.92; burst onsets,
# sample-exact), then deleted.
# 'burst interval across it': the interval between the last burst before the change and
# the first after it, off whole seconds of the source: a jump of OBS's audio timeline.
# 'offset': burst onset minus the flash's frame, per flash (positive: audio late).
#
# Reading: video reopens: 0.00 ms. Audio reopens: -10.54 ms and +10.73 ms, about
# 512 samples either way: a step, not a reset to where OBS's offset began. Between
# changes the offset stays put: OBS lays the samples back to back (audio-timeline 0.00
# ppm), and the 60 Hz canvas samples the 60.0016 Hz source with the same rate error, so
# the two cancel in the file until the canvas skips a source frame (about every 10.75
# min: the 42.0/58.6 flicker 40-60 s after change 4, then +16.7 ms). The -16.7 ms between
# segments 0 and 1 is the test source's (its offset at the driver: +42.11 ms before OBS,
# +25.45 ms after).

